    <qti-feedback-block identifier="show_interaction" outcome-identifier="INTERACTION_VISIBILITY" show-hide="show" class="qti-margin-0">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>Which sentence uses &quot;archetype&quot; correctly?</p>
    <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="RESPONSE" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">Folklorists treat the flood story as an archetype: societies on every continent tell of waters that rise, one warned family, and a world begun again.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="B">The vineyard&apos;s archetype — a gnarled cutting planted back in 1911 — still parents every single one of the vines growing across the hillside today.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="C">Voters found the candidate a refreshing archetype, unlike anyone who had ever held the office before her.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="D">The consulting firm&apos;s archetype forbids checked luggage on any one-day trip, and the travel auditors are famously strict about enforcing it.</qti-simple-choice>
    </qti-choice-interaction>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>

    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-A" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct! An archetype is the original, recurring pattern of a type, so it&apos;s the correct answer because the flood story keeps reappearing — rising waters, one warned family, a world begun again — across societies that never shared a page.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-B" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! An archetype is the original, recurring pattern of a type, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because a cutting planted in 1911 is a physical ancestor — a parent specimen, not a pattern that later tellings keep refilling.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-C" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! An archetype is the original, recurring pattern of a type, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because a candidate &apos;unlike anyone who had ever held the office&apos; matches no pattern — the sentence describes a break from type, not the type itself.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-D" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! An archetype is the original, recurring pattern of a type, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because a rule about checked luggage is a policy — something a firm enforces, not a figure that stories keep reproducing.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>
